      <h1>Acceptance Testing: Verifying Incoming Batteries</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Acceptance testing verifies a <strong>batch of incoming batteries against
        the specification before installation</strong>. The standard sequence: <strong>visual checks</strong>
        (case, terminals, labels), <strong>date-code check</strong> (shelf age), <strong>open-circuit
        voltage</strong> (state of charge on arrival), and a <strong>spot conductance or load test</strong> on a
        sample. It catches damage and shelf-aging before they become field failures.
      </div>

      <h2>The incoming-QC sequence</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What it catches</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>1. Visual</strong></td><td>Case damage, terminal defects, label mismatch</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>2. Date code</strong></td><td>Shelf age (see <a href="https://worldbatteryhub.com/lead-acid/battery-date-code/">date codes</a>)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>3. OCV</strong></td><td>State of charge on arrival</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>4. Spot test (sample)</strong></td><td>Conductance or <a href="/load-testing/">load test</a> on a sample</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why it pays for itself</h2>
      <p>
        A battery that arrives shelf-aged or damaged fails in service — often at the worst moment, at site cost
        far above the battery's price. The acceptance sequence takes minutes per unit and catches the defects
        while the supplier is still responsible (see
        <a href="https://technology.worldbatteryhub.com/quality-control/">manufacturing QC</a> for the factory-side
        version of the same logic).
      </p>

      <h2>What to do with failures</h2>
      <p>
        A failed acceptance test is a <strong>supplier conversation, not a repair</strong>: document the reading,
        quarantine the unit, and reject per the purchase terms. The pattern matters too — isolated failures are
        logistics damage; repeated failures across a batch point to a supplier or shipping problem worth
        escalating.
      </p>

      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My take:</strong> Acceptance testing is the cheapest quality insurance a battery buyer has — a
        five-minute incoming check converts supplier problems from field emergencies into paperwork — and its
        real value is the pattern: one failure is noise, a batch of failures is a supplier signal.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> The failure curve is front-loaded: defects that will strand a site usually show
        themselves at arrival, before installation. Testing at the dock is where the buyer still has leverage —
        afterwards, the supplier's responsibility and the buyer's options both shrink.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

